# Injection and untrusted sinks

Validate at the boundary. Encode at the sink. A check in the client is not
the control. Floor controls (parameterized queries, argv over shell, path
allowlists, SSRF allowlist, redirects, uploads, no untrusted deserialize) live
in `security-baseline`. This rule adds sink depth.

## Queries and commands

- SQL and other datastores: bind parameters or use the project's query
  builder. Do not concatenate user text into the query
- Do not merge a client JSON object into a query operator (`$where`, `$gt`,
  and similar) without an allowlist of fields
- Commands: pass an argument array. Do not interpolate into a shell string
- LDAP, XPath, and header values: escape for that syntax. Strip CR and LF
  from values that become headers or mail headers

## Markup, templates, and objects

- HTML, attributes, JavaScript, and URLs each need their own encoding
- Do not let a user choose a template name, a format string, or a view path
- Do not deserialize untrusted pickle, YAML, Java object streams, or similar
  object graphs. Prefer a plain data format with a schema
- Bound or reject regular expressions taken from users. Untrusted patterns
  can hang the process

## Requests, paths, and files

- Server-side fetches: allowlist the host. Resolve the address, then refuse
  loopback, link-local, and cloud metadata. Disable automatic redirects, or
  re-validate host and resolved address on every hop before following
- Redirects: allowlist destinations. A user-controlled `next` parameter is an
  open redirect unless it is checked
- Paths: resolve under an allowlisted root and reject `..`, absolute paths,
  and symlink escapes
- Uploads: limit size and type; sniff type from content when the platform
  allows; store under a generated name outside the executable or web root;
  ignore client filename and content type. Do not serve the upload with a
  type that the browser will execute. Set `nosniff` on the download response
  when adding a new public download path
